Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
NO MORE FUNDING:
Traditionally, the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:08.120
one goal for any company is to maximize profit&nbsp;
for shareholders. But, for quite some time now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:13.268
we’ve seen a new breed of companies who don’t&nbsp;
care at all about profit. In fact, profit isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.268 --> 00:00:15.320
even part of the equation. It’s all about growth.&nbsp;
If these companies are able to show large enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:22.360
user growth figures, nothing else matters. And I’m&nbsp;
not just talking about random background startups.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:28.040
Many of the most recognizable startups in the&nbsp;
world have never made a single penny. Zillow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:35.360
for example, loses around $200 million per year.&nbsp;
Snapchat loses $1.3 billion per year. Lyft also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:42.520
loses $1.3 billion per year. But no one is as bad&nbsp;
as Uber who till just 6 months ago was losing $9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:48.600
billion per year. One of the main reasons for this&nbsp;
is the insane growth is everything perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.760
of modern investors. They’re willing to stomach&nbsp;
insane losses for not just a year or two but 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:02.440
10, 15 years. But, it seems that things are&nbsp;
changing and they’re changing fast. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:09.920
funding has fallen off a cliff across the board.&nbsp;
In Q1 of 2023, global funding had fallen to $76&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.560
billion which is a 53% decline from the $162&nbsp;
billion that it was the year prior. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.760
was despite two massive deals with OpenAI and&nbsp;
Stripe who raised $10 billion and $6.5 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.720
respectively. Without those deals, funding&nbsp;
would’ve been down to $60 billion or over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.720
$100 billion less than 2022. You would think that&nbsp;
think that the startups that are affected the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.440
are the early stage ones that are just getting&nbsp;
started as the risk with these companies are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:43.440
highest. But, it turns out that the startups that&nbsp;
are being affected the most are actually the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:50.160
stage startups with a chronic money burning&nbsp;
problem. As such, most of these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.760
are scrambling to get their act together. Uber,&nbsp;
for example has been magically able reduce their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.080
losses from $9 billion per year to just $300&nbsp;
million per year. But, not all startups are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:07.240
having such an easy time as they’re realizing&nbsp;
that becoming profitable isn’t as easy as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:12.840
originally thought. So, join me as we take a look&nbsp;
back at how this new breed of companies even came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:26.303
to be and what this funding cut means for some&nbsp;
of the most well known startups in the world.

00:02:26.303 --> 00:02:28.360
