00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.480
it seems that you have to be willing to burn&nbsp;
money for atleast the first 5 or 10 years but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.960
this is completely false. In fact, this was never&nbsp;
a thing with traditional tech companies. Intel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.400
for example, became profitable just 3 years after&nbsp;
being founded. Apple became profitable just 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:50.760
years after being founded. And even Google, a&nbsp;
more modern tech company only took 3 years to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.760
reach profitability. Also, it wasn’t like these&nbsp;
companies were burning money on user acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.680
or marketing or insane amounts of overhead.&nbsp;
Rather, they were just investing in getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.720
their first product to market, and as soon as they&nbsp;
were able to release their first product, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:11.320
were able to become profitable. But all of this&nbsp;
would change with a company called Amazon. Unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:16.600
the tech entrepreneurs that came before him, Jeff&nbsp;
had a different perspective. Instead of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.200
sell a superior product for a premium price&nbsp;
which makes it easy to achieve profitability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.080
Jeff wanted to center Amazon around killing&nbsp;
margins. His catch phrase was literally “Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:34.040
margin is my opportunity.” But, while this sounds&nbsp;
nice on paper, it’s not as clean in application as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:39.960
this usually just turns into a race to the bottom.&nbsp;
And that’s exactly what happened. Most of us only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.960
remember Amazon and eBay but that’s not to say&nbsp;
that they were the only players in the e commerce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.720
market. They were simply the only ones who&nbsp;
survived. The reality was that dozens of players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.960
entered the e commerce market strapped with dotcom&nbsp;
funding. This included companies like boo.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.960 --> 00:05:03.280
Webvan, eToys, and pets.com. To their credit, many&nbsp;
of these companies actually become quite large.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.800
Webvan, for example, was a grocery delivery&nbsp;
business that’s eerily similar to Instacart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.640
Thanks to dotcom funding, they were able to expand&nbsp;
to 26 cities, hire 3,500 employees, and even have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:21.080
a billion dollar IPO. But, scale wasn’t the only&nbsp;
thing that these companies had in common. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:28.320
of them were also burning ridiculous amounts of&nbsp;
money. Boo.com was burning through $135 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:35.240
within 18 months. Pets.com was burning through&nbsp;
$300 million within 2 years. And as for Webvan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.640
they were actually pulling in a good amount&nbsp;
of revenue: $178 million, but their expenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:50.200
were wait for it $525 million. This meant that for&nbsp;
every dollar they pulled in, they spent 3 dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.920
And it wasn’t on building out the product&nbsp;
or expansion or anything like that. Almost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:01.600
all of the expenses of all of these companies&nbsp;
was just marketing. In fact, Boo.com spent $25&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.520
million on advertising before they even opened.&nbsp;
The cost of the product itself was miniscule for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.640
these companies compared to their other expenses.&nbsp;
One of Boo.com’s main technologies for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:20.280
only cost $6 million to develop but $135 million&nbsp;
to market. With these sort of economies, it’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.240
wonder why things eventually came crumbling down&nbsp;
with the dotcom bust when all of these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:31.120
went under. After this disaster though, it seemed&nbsp;
like investors had finally learned their lesson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:37.600
Economics don’t work differently just because it’s&nbsp;
a shiny tech company. But somehow, 20 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:51.537
we would end up in the exact same position&nbsp;
once again. So, what in the world happened?

00:06:51.537 --> 00:06:54.200
EXCEPTIONS MAKE A CASE:
Likely the number one culprit that normalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:59.800
money losing tech endeavors once again is big&nbsp;
tech starting with of course Amazon. After the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:05.840
dotcom bubble burst, Amazon was feeling the pain&nbsp;
just like every other dotcom company but had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:11.280
rather unique solution. Instead of just trying to&nbsp;
focus on profitability with their retail business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.920
they decided to just open a whole new business&nbsp;
called AWS to pay for the unprofitabilty of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:23.560
retail business. So, essentially, they became&nbsp;
their own funders and I think we all know how this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:30.040
played out. Amazon would end up growing 500x and&nbsp;
become synonymous with online retail despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:35.080
fact that they were never able to consistently&nbsp;
turn a profit to this day. Seeing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:39.760
many investors many investors started feeling&nbsp;
a bit of FOMO. Maybe, they were right about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:46.320
dotcom companies all along. Maybe all they needed&nbsp;
was more time and funding to succeed. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:52.600
it took 20-30 years for Amazon to reach their&nbsp;
stride. If Amazon was the only instance, things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:58.040
wouldn’t have gotten so out of hand but they&nbsp;
weren’t which brings us into our next culprit:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:02.760
Google. Ever since Google became profitable,&nbsp;
they’ve built a career out of building no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:08.680
margin/negative margin products. This includes&nbsp;
Gmail, YouTube, Maps, Android, Google Cloud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:14.000
the Pixel and basically anything else that comes&nbsp;
out of Google. Their strategy is to simply reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.120
as many people as possible with these products&nbsp;
and monetize all of them using Google search and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.960
Google ads. Once again, this ended up playing&nbsp;
out extraordinarily well as Google would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:31.400
up growing 50X as they grew multiple ubiquitous&nbsp;
platforms with billions of users each. But all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:37.737
this was nothing in comparison to what Zuckerberg&nbsp;
would do. Amazon and Google were simply subsidzing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.737 --> 00:08:43.080
inhouse products with other in house products.&nbsp;
Zuckerberg, however, would go out of his way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:49.000
to buy money pits just for the users. This of&nbsp;
course included Instagram for $1 billion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:55.160
WhatsApp for $19 billion. While Facebook got a lot&nbsp;
of flack for these acquisitions back in the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:01.880
these ended up becoming cash cows. Instagram is&nbsp;
now valued at over a $100 billion and WhatsApp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:06.920
is likely worth even more. These big tech&nbsp;
companies were clearly making a strong case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:11.560
for pursuing money burning endeavors but the&nbsp;
factor that pushed investors over the edge was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:17.920
the exponential startups of the late 2000s. This&nbsp;
of course starts with Uber. Uber was founded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:25.280
March of 2009 and is still not profitable but&nbsp;
it’s worth $90 billion. Lyft was founded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:33.640
2012 and is still not profitable but it’s worth&nbsp;
$4 billion even after falling 90%. Doordash was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:39.960
founded in 2013 and is still not profitable&nbsp;
but it’s worth $30 billion even after falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:48.280
50%. Stripe was founded in 2010, and is only now&nbsp;
becoming profitable but it’s already worth a $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:57.440
billion. I can go on forever. Robinhood, Coinbase,&nbsp;
GrubHub, AirBnB, MongoDB, Zoom, Affirm, Carvana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:03.120
Twilio, SnapChat, Octa, Rivian, Palantir, Peloton.&nbsp;
Basically any startup that you recognize and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.560
founded within the past 15 years likely falls&nbsp;
within the category as dozens if not hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:14.160
of money burning startups were founded in the late&nbsp;
2000s and early 2010s and went on to be phenoemnal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:20.880
investments despite never turning a profit. With&nbsp;
each and every passing year, investors and VCs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:29.040
were becoming just a little bit more tolerant&nbsp;
of losses leading us straight into the pandemic.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:36.240
DOTCOM BUBBLE 2.0:
When the pandemic hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:43.320
investor FOMO went into overdrive. Tech companies&nbsp;
seemed like a gold mine as everyone was sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.880
at home spending all of their time on phones,&nbsp;
computers, or TVs. All of the big tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.120
started hiring like crazy as they thought&nbsp;
this was it, this was the new frontier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.120 --> 00:11:00.040
the new era of tech domination. Meta nearly&nbsp;
doubled their employee count from 44,000 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:05.880
nearly 90,000. Google also nearly doubled their&nbsp;
employee count from just over a 100,000 to nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:13.840
200,000. Big tech stocks and pandemic stocks woud&nbsp;
go insane and VCs couldn’t help but feel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:20.760
they had missed. And that’s when they decided,&nbsp;
they may have missed out on this last run up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:25.760
but they sure as hell weren’t gonna miss&nbsp;
out on the next run up. And with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:30.000
they started pummeling money into basically any&nbsp;
startup that come through the door with insane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.640
valuations out of the date. And I think this graph&nbsp;
really showcases the essence of what was going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:43.240
Back in 2012, there were a healthy 5-10 unicorns&nbsp;
every quarter. This increased to 20 to 40 in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:49.920
mid 2010s before rising to 50 to 100 in the late&nbsp;
2010s. And then finally, it would explode to 220&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:56.840
unicorns per quarter in 2021. That’s the same&nbsp;
as over 2 unicorns every single day and keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:03.120
in mind the stock market is only open 252 days&nbsp;
per year. So really, 3 companies were reaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:10.240
unicorn status on any given day in 2021. And make&nbsp;
no mistake, this has nothing to do with rising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:16.480
innovation or more opportunity. The only thing&nbsp;
this has to do with is VC funding and investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:24.960
greed. I mean, just look at this graph. Funding&nbsp;
literally grew 10X between 2011 and 2021. But, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:31.120
good news is that this madness is finally coming&nbsp;
to an end which just leaves the question: what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:37.360
happens next? Well, it’s really simple honestly. A&nbsp;
large number of money burning startups will simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:43.280
go bankrupt. And thi shas already started to&nbsp;
playout. Within just the first half of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:50.720
338 US companies filed for bankruptcy. 54 of these&nbsp;
companies had VC or private equity backing meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:56.840
that if this trend keeps up, over 100 startups&nbsp;
will go bankrupt by the end of this year alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:02.000
And as interest rates continue to stay high, the&nbsp;
economic pain for these startups will simply get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:07.120
worse and worse. Most of the startups that manage&nbsp;
to fend off bankruptcy for another year or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:12.000
will likely be bought out by larger companies&nbsp;
for rock bottom valuations. If things play out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:17.960
similar to the dotcom crash, only half of them&nbsp;
will make it past this initial purge. And most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:22.240
the companies that make it through will be a shell&nbsp;
of their former selves with no way back to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:27.720
all time highs. If we look back at their stock&nbsp;
graph in 20 years, it’ll probably look something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:32.920
like this. Of course, a select few companies will&nbsp;
defy the odds and not only return to their former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:38.560
highs but make new highs. These companies will&nbsp;
become the Googles, Facebooks, and Amazons of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:45.400
the next generation. But for everyone else though,&nbsp;
this is pretty much the end of the road as the VC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:51.640
money printer has finally stopped. Google is&nbsp;
definitely feeling a lot of this pain but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:55.440
seems like they’ve got a pretty good strategy&nbsp;
to pivot. Check out this video to learn more.

